# `data[w]` appears in the loop header and in the loop body. The holder and the key are module-level consts that the compiler knows nothing about.
# If the holder is a plain object and the key is a string that names an own data property, the read runs no code.
# The iterations write no named property of any object, so the read finds the same value in every iteration.
# The read is therefore hoisted: it is done once, before the specialized version of the loop, by an operation that runs no code and returns absent in every other case.
# If the operation returns absent, the generic version of the loop runs. A hundred iterations went from 10,106 instructions to 836. Plain Bun takes 915.
# Here the key names an accessor, so twelve iterations call the getter twenty-five times.
# Fault injection, testing=UseGenericOwnKeyReads: the hoisted read takes the generic path, which runs code. The getter is called fewer times, silently.
# RUN(EVENT): compile InvariantKeyReadsHoisted mechanisms=all
